          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-2a9b6c844f229aa3\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Sonya directly avows that she loves the countess and the whole Rostov family. The utterance primarily presents her loving emotional state as true, partly to rebut the charge that she is ungrateful. Although spoken amid intense crying, it is a coherent, contextually reasoned avowal rather than a reflex-like verbal discharge. The adjacent willingness to sacrifice everything expresses devotion but does not turn the highlighted love assertion itself into a relational undertaking.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Sonya directly addresses Natasha while referring to the countess as “her” and to Natasha and the Rostov household collectively as “all of you.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Distressed about the obstacles to marrying Nikolai and Vera’s accusation that she is ungrateful, Sonya invokes her love for the countess and the Rostov family as evidence of gratitude and loyalty. Her oath-like framing, crossing herself, tears, and subsequent claim that she would sacrifice everything intensify and support the sincerity of the avowal, but do not independently establish performative or expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“я так люблю и ее, и всех вас” (“I love her so much, and all of you too”).\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense wording explicitly avows Sonya’s love for the countess and the Rostov family, strongly supporting truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Sonya anticipates being accused of having “no heart” and being “ungrateful,” then swears “вот ей-Богу,” crosses herself, and states that she loves the countess and all of them.\",\"supports\":\"The declaration functions as a reasoned rebuttal of ingratitude and an emphatic assertion of her actual feelings, rather than as a new undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Sonya has recovered some strength before speaking; after the declaration she says she is so grateful that she would gladly sacrifice everything, and then becomes unable to continue.\",\"supports\":\"The emotional delivery is intense, but the organized explanation and distinct adjacent statement of sacrificial willingness favor an avowal over reflex-like expressive discharge or commitment encoded by the highlighted words.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as affirmative, present-tense, canonical, and retained as a valid explicit love-I-you instance.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms identification of the highlighted phrase as the relevant explicit love utterance.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"Truth-conditional avowal adequately represents the core force. The surrounding distress, oath, gratitude, and sacrificial devotion are contextual features and do not create residual pressure for P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The grammatical object is coordinated and mixed-person: Sonya loves “her” (the countess) and “all of you” (the Rostov family, including Natasha).\"}"
